BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bpea-financing-2026@nmai-research-calendar
DTSTAMP:20260924T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Brookings discussion of AI financing and policy
DESCRIPTION:Which assumptions and exposure gaps survive the research discus
 sion?\nInspect: Conference draft\, presentation and responses concerning i
 nvestment scenarios\, financing structures and the visibility of creditor 
 exposures.\nImprovement: Better measurements and explicit sensitivity chec
 ks narrow uncertainty.\nDeterioration: Material exposure gaps persist or a
 ssumptions are contradicted by disclosed contracts and cash flows.\nLimits
 : A research presentation is not a financial outcome or a forecast of coll
 apse.\nCheck date: 2026-09-24\nOutcome status: not_observed\nhttps://www.b
 rookings.edu/events/bpea-fall-2026-conference/
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bea-q2-2026@nmai-research-calendar
DTSTAMP:20260924T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:US Q2 GDP revision\, industry data and August household income
DESCRIPTION:Is growth broadening\, and can households sustain demand?\nInsp
 ect: Industry contributions\, revisions\, real disposable income\, real co
 nsumption and saving. Adjust investment interpretation for imports.\nImpro
 vement: Broader domestic demand and improving real household income.\nDete
 rioration: Growth concentrated in investment alongside weakening real inco
 me or consumption.\nLimits: A revision describes an earlier quarter\; it i
 s not a September activity reading or a clean AI counterfactual.\nCheck da
 te: 2026-09-24\nOutcome status: not_observed\nhttps://www.bea.gov/news/sch
 edule/full
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:micron-q4-2026@nmai-research-calendar
DTSTAMP:20260924T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Micron fiscal Q4 results
DESCRIPTION:Do reported orders and any customer prepayments lead to deliver
 ed supply and collected cash?\nInspect: Cash flow\, any disclosed customer
  deposits and their repayment or financing terms\, delivered memory and re
 vised capacity plans.\nImprovement: Disclosed cash receipts and delivery c
 onsistent with contractual milestones.\nDeterioration: Deposit reversals\,
  delivery slippage or financing needs growing faster than operating receip
 ts.\nLimits: An earnings call does not independently confirm customer acce
 ptance\; management forecasts remain forecasts.\nCheck date: 2026-09-24\nO
 utcome status: not_observed\nhttps://investors.micron.com/news/press-relea
 se/2026/Micron-Technology-to-Report-Fiscal-Fourth-Quarter-Results-on-Septe
 mber-30-2026/default.aspx
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:jobs-sep-2026@nmai-research-calendar
DTSTAMP:20260924T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261002
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261003
SUMMARY:US September employment report
DESCRIPTION:Are hiring conditions improving beyond a few sectors?\nInspect:
  Payroll revisions\, unemployment\, participation\, hours and industry det
 ail.\nImprovement: Hiring and hours improve across industries without fall
 ing participation concealing weakness.\nDeterioration: Downward revisions 
 and weaker hiring or hours across several sectors.\nLimits: US aggregate e
 mployment does not measure EU AI operations vacancies.\nCheck date: 2026-0
 9-24\nOutcome status: not_observed\nhttps://www.bls.gov/schedule/2026/10_s
 ched.htm
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eia-oct-2026@nmai-research-calendar
DTSTAMP:20260924T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261006
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261007
SUMMARY:EIA energy outlook and Winter Fuels Outlook
DESCRIPTION:How do observed physical supply conditions compare with the lat
 est price forecast?\nInspect: Changes in production\, inventories\, export
 -route assumptions\, refining and household fuel costs.\nImprovement: Obse
 rved supply\, inventories and household costs support a less constrained o
 utlook.\nDeterioration: Supply constraints or inventory declines undermine
  the forecast.\nLimits: An official forecast remains a forecast. Falling o
 il prices can also reflect weaker demand.\nCheck date: 2026-09-24\nOutcome
  status: not_observed\nhttps://www.eia.gov/outlooks/steo/release_schedule.
 php
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asml-q3-2026@nmai-research-calendar
DTSTAMP:20260924T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261014
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261015
SUMMARY:ASML Q3 results
DESCRIPTION:Are equipment orders converting into recognised sales and insta
 llations?\nInspect: Bookings\, cancellations\, shipments\, customer accept
 ance\, receivables and export restrictions.\nImprovement: Delivered equipm
 ent and cash collection support earlier orders.\nDeterioration: Cancellati
 ons\, acceptance delays or restrictive permissions affect delivery.\nLimit
 s: Bookings are not cash or installed productive capacity\; product and re
 gional mix matter.\nCheck date: 2026-09-24\nOutcome status: not_observed\n
 https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/937966/000162828026048235/statutor
 yinterimreport20.htm
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iea-oct-2026@nmai-research-calendar
DTSTAMP:20260924T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261014
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261015
SUMMARY:IEA October Oil Market Report
DESCRIPTION:Do the IEA and EIA readings agree on the direction of physical 
 energy conditions?\nInspect: Observed stocks\, production\, refining and t
 rade alongside revisions to forecasts.\nImprovement: Physical indicators a
 gree on improving supply conditions across sources.\nDeterioration: Stock 
 draws or route disruption persist despite a more optimistic forecast.\nLim
 its: The detailed report may require a subscription\; record unavailable t
 ables rather than infer them from highlights.\nCheck date: 2026-09-24\nOut
 come status: not_observed\nhttps://www.iea.org/events/oil-market-report-oc
 tober-2026
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:inflation-sep-2026@nmai-research-calendar
DTSTAMP:20260924T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261014
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261015
SUMMARY:US September CPI and real earnings
DESCRIPTION:How are energy prices affecting underlying costs and household 
 purchasing power?\nInspect: Headline and underlying components\, real earn
 ings and prior-period comparisons.\nImprovement: Energy pressure eases wit
 hout broadening to other components\, alongside firmer real earnings.\nDet
 erioration: Energy pressure persists and broadens while real earnings weak
 en.\nLimits: One monthly print does not establish a trend or determine the
  next interest-rate decision.\nCheck date: 2026-09-24\nOutcome status: not
 _observed\nhttps://www.bls.gov/schedule/2026/10_sched.htm
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tsmc-q3-2026@nmai-research-calendar
DTSTAMP:20260924T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261015
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261016
SUMMARY:TSMC Q3 results
DESCRIPTION:Is chip demand supported by delivered output rather than orders
  alone?\nInspect: Reported revenue\, capacity utilisation where disclosed\
 , capital spending\, cash generation and next-period outlook.\nImprovement
 : Delivered demand and cash generation remain consistent with capacity add
 itions.\nDeterioration: Customer concentration\, postponed orders or utili
 sation weakness emerge.\nLimits: Supplier strength does not establish prof
 itability for the AI customers buying its output.\nCheck date: 2026-09-24\
 nOutcome status: not_observed\nhttps://investor.cld.tsmc.com/english
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fed-oct-2026@nmai-research-calendar
DTSTAMP:20260924T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261028
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261029
SUMMARY:FOMC decision after 27-28 October meeting
DESCRIPTION:How does policy alter financing exposure?\nInspect: Decision an
 d rationale\; then compare borrower floating-rate terms\, credit margins a
 nd hedges.\nImprovement: Financing costs or risk premiums ease without sev
 ere demand deterioration.\nDeterioration: Benchmark rates or lender margin
 s rise for exposed borrowers.\nLimits: The policy rate is not each borrowe
 r's all-in cost. Fixed-rate debt does not instantly reprice.\nCheck date: 
 2026-09-24\nOutcome status: not_observed\nhttps://www.federalreserve.gov/m
 onetarypolicy/fomccalendars.htm
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bea-q3-2026@nmai-research-calendar
DTSTAMP:20260924T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261029
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261030
SUMMARY:US Q3 advance GDP and September income/outlays
DESCRIPTION:Is investment-led growth translating into broader demand?\nInsp
 ect: Real final domestic demand\, investment components\, imports and real
  income.\nImprovement: Growth broadens and households retain purchasing po
 wer.\nDeterioration: Investment slows while household demand weakens.\nLim
 its: Advance estimates are revised\; no dedicated national-accounts AI cat
 egory isolates the causal effect.\nCheck date: 2026-09-24\nOutcome status:
  not_observed\nhttps://www.bea.gov/news/schedule/full
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
